# Adding a New Module — NutritionCounter

Follow every step in order. Replace `<Name>` with the module name (e.g. `Analytics`).

---

## 1. Source & Resources directories

```
Modules/<Name>/Sources/             ← all production Swift files
Modules/<Name>/Resources/           ← module-level resources
Modules/<Name>/Resources/en.lproj/Localizable.strings  ← English strings
Modules/<Name>/Resources/he.lproj/Localizable.strings  ← Hebrew strings
```

Seed both `.strings` files with the same keys. English values match the key; Hebrew
values are the translated text:

```
/* Description of the string. */
"My Key" = "My Key";
```

```
/* Description of the string. */
"My Key" = "המפתח שלי";
```

After running `tuist generate`, Tuist auto-generates
`Derived/Sources/TuistStrings+Nutrition<Name>.swift` containing a
`Nutrition<Name>Strings` enum with typed `String` properties for every key.

Add string keys only for literals used directly within `Modules/<Name>/Sources/`.
Never duplicate a key that already lives in another module's strings.

## 2. Framework target — `Tuist/ProjectDescriptionHelpers/Targets/Nutrition<Name>+Target.swift`

```swift
import ProjectDescription

public extension Target {
    static var nutrition<Name>: Target {
        .module(
            name: "Nutrition<Name>",
            sources: "Modules/<Name>/Sources/**/*.swift",
            resources: ["Modules/<Name>/Resources/**"],
            dependencies: [
                .target(name: "NutritionCore"),   // add/remove as needed
            ]
        )
    }
}
```

> The `resources:` glob picks up every `.lproj` folder. Tuist auto-generates
> `Derived/Sources/TuistStrings+Nutrition<Name>.swift` with a
> `Nutrition<Name>Strings` enum — use it as `Text(Nutrition<Name>Strings.myKey)`.

## 3. Unit test target — `Tuist/ProjectDescriptionHelpers/Targets/Nutrition<Name>Tests+Target.swift`

```swift
import ProjectDescription

public extension Target {
    static var nutrition<Name>Tests: Target {
        .target(
            name: "Nutrition<Name>Tests",
            destinations: .iOS,
            product: .unitTests,
            bundleId: "\(ProjectConstants.bundleIdPrefix).Nutrition<Name>Tests",
            deploymentTargets: ProjectConstants.deploymentTarget,
            sources: ["Tests/Nutrition<Name>Tests/**/*.swift"],
            dependencies: [
                .target(name: "Nutrition<Name>"),
                .target(name: "NutritionCore"),
                .package(product: "Cuckoo"),
            ]
        )
    }
}
```

## 4. System test target — `Tuist/ProjectDescriptionHelpers/Targets/Nutrition<Name>SystemTests+Target.swift`

```swift
import ProjectDescription

public extension Target {
    static var nutrition<Name>SystemTests: Target {
        .target(
            name: "Nutrition<Name>SystemTests",
            destinations: .iOS,
            product: .unitTests,          // unitTests (not uiTests) — KIF is in-process
            bundleId: "\(ProjectConstants.bundleIdPrefix).Nutrition<Name>SystemTests",
            deploymentTargets: ProjectConstants.deploymentTarget,
            sources: ["Tests/Nutrition<Name>SystemTests/**/*.swift"],
            dependencies: [
                .target(name: "NutritionCounter"),      // host app for BUNDLE_LOADER/TEST_HOST
                .target(name: "NutritionTestsHelper"),  // SystemTestCase + KIF (link KIF only here, not again below)
            ],
            settings: .settings(base: ["OTHER_LDFLAGS": "$(inherited) -ObjC"])
        )
    }
}
```

## 5. Test source directories & files

```
Tests/Nutrition<Name>Tests/
    <Feature>Tests.swift        ← XCTestCase unit tests (GIVEN/WHEN/THEN)
    GeneratedMocks.swift        ← placeholder; Cuckoo overwrites at build time

Tests/Nutrition<Name>SystemTests/
    <Feature>SystemTests.swift  ← final class … : SystemTestCase { tester()… }
```

- Unit tests: `import Cuckoo` + `@testable import Nutrition<Name>`
- System tests: `import NutritionTestsHelper` only — gives `SystemTestCase` + full KIF surface

## 6. Cuckoofile.toml — add a module block if Core protocols need mocking

```toml
[modules.NutritionCoreFor<Name>]
output = "Tests/Nutrition<Name>Tests/GeneratedMocks.swift"
testableImports = ["NutritionCore"]
sources = ["Modules/Core/Sources/**/*.swift"]

[modules.NutritionCoreFor<Name>.options]
protocolsOnly = true
```

Also add the new output path to `NutritionCoreTests+Target.swift`'s `outputPaths` list so the single Cuckoo generator run produces all files.

## 7. Project.swift — register all four targets

```swift
// MARK: - Nutrition<Name> tests
.nutrition<Name>,
.nutrition<Name>Tests,
.nutrition<Name>SystemTests,
```

## 8. Regenerate

```bash
tuist generate
```

---

## Localization rules

| Rule | Reason |
|---|---|
| Each module owns the strings used in its `Sources/` | Prevents duplicate keys across bundles |
| Use the Tuist-generated enum (`Text(Nutrition<Name>Strings.myKey)`) | Compile-time safety; avoids runtime key typos |
| Never duplicate a key that already lives in a dependency's strings | Static frameworks merge their bundles into the host — duplicates cause conflicts |
| Never add a key to the main app's strings that belongs to a module | The main app catalog is for app-level-only strings |
| The widget (`NutritionAppWidget`) keeps its own `Localizable.xcstrings` | Widget extensions are a separate target with their own bundle |

### Using strings from a dependency

Each Tuist-generated enum (e.g. `NutritionCoreStrings`) is `public`, so any module
that depends on it can reference its properties directly:

```swift
// In NutritionHome (which depends on NutritionCore):
Text(NutritionCoreStrings.protein)
```

**Example — domain-level strings in NutritionCore:**

```swift
// NutrientType.swift — uses Tuist-generated NutritionCoreStrings enum.
public var localizedName: String {
    switch self {
    case .protein: NutritionCoreStrings.protein
    case .carbs:   NutritionCoreStrings.carbs
    case .fat:     NutritionCoreStrings.fat
    }
}
```

When deciding where a new string key belongs, ask:
> "Which is the deepest dependency that every consumer of this string already depends on?"

Put the key there. Prefer `NutritionCore` for domain concepts (nutrient names, unit labels)
and module-specific strings for UI copy (error messages, button titles, accessibility labels).

### Format strings

For strings with arguments (e.g. `"Decrease %@"`), Tuist generates a function:

```swift
// In Localizable.strings:
// "Decrease %@" = "Decrease %1$@";
//
// Generated:
// public static func decrease(_ p1: Any) -> String

Text(NutritionUIStrings.decrease(nutrient.localizedName))
```

## Key rules

| Rule | Reason |
|---|---|
| `product: .unitTests` for KIF targets | KIF is in-process; `.uiTests` is out-of-process XCUITest |
| `OTHER_LDFLAGS: -ObjC` on system test targets | Forces Obj-C categories (KIF extensions on UIView, XCTestCase) to load |
| Never `.package(product: "KIF")` on system tests | KIF is already inside `NutritionTestsHelper`; linking twice → **duplicate symbol** errors |
| Inherit `SystemTestCase`, not `KIFTestCase` | `tester` is an Obj-C macro invisible to Swift; `SystemTestCase` provides the Swift wrapper |
| One Cuckoo generate script (in `NutritionCoreTests`) | Generates all `GeneratedMocks.swift` files in one pass to avoid races |

Consider these rules if they affect your changes.
